<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hohhot</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Guihua</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hohhot is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a> Autonomous Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the plain north of the great bend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>. The area was an early centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a>, and the city was founded in the 1570s by the Tumed ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altan-khan">Altan Khan</a>, receiving the Chinese name Guihua. It grew around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhist</a> monasteries such as the Dazhao.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hohhot.jpg" alt="Hohhot" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jonashtand, CC BY-SA 4.0 &lt; via Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20240812_Arriving_in_Hohhot_ROTATEEDIT.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
